---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import MoonSim from '../../simulators/moon/MoonSim.astro';
import { moonQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/moon';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why the Moon has phases' },
  { id: 'cycle', label: 'The cycle of phases' },
  { id: 'rising', label: 'When the Moon rises' },
  { id: 'hemispheres', label: 'North vs. south' },
  { id: 'eclipses', label: 'Phases are not eclipses' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formulas' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world connections' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Section 4.5: Phases and Motions of the Moon. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-5-phases-and-motions-of-the-moon' },
  { text: 'Fraknoi, A., Morrison, D., & Wolff, S. C. (2022). Astronomy 2e, Section 4.7: Eclipses of the Sun and Moon. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/4-7-eclipses-of-the-sun-and-moon' },
  { text: 'NASA Science. Moon Phases.', url: 'https://science.nasa.gov/moon/moon-phases/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="astronomy"
  topic="moon-phases"
  description="Interactive Moon phases simulator: move the Moon around Earth and see each phase from above and from the ground, with illumination, moonrise times and hemisphere views."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={moonQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Night after night the Moon changes shape — a thin crescent, a half disc, a bright full moon — and then back again.
      The Moon itself never changes; what changes is our point of view. Move the Moon around its orbit and see the
      geometry behind every phase.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <MoonSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>The left side shows the Sun, Earth and Moon <strong>from above the North Pole</strong>. The right side shows the Moon <strong>as you see it from Earth</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Drag the Moon</strong> around its orbit, use the slider, or press a phase button. Press <strong>Start</strong> to run through a whole month.</li>
      <li>Notice that the Moon in the left view is <strong>always half lit</strong> — only the right-hand view changes.</li>
      <li>Read the <strong>phase, lit portion, moonrise time</strong> and best viewing time below.</li>
      <li>Switch to the <strong>Southern Hemisphere</strong> and turn on <strong>Earth’s shadow</strong> to test common ideas. <strong>Reset</strong> returns to new moon.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why the Moon has phases</h2>
  <p>
    The Moon does not make its own light. It shines because it reflects sunlight, and at every moment the Sun lights
    exactly <strong>half</strong> of it — the half facing the Sun. As the Moon travels around Earth, the angle between
    the Sun, Earth and Moon changes, so we see different amounts of that sunlit half. When the lit half faces away from
    us we see almost nothing; when it faces us, we see a full disc.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cycle">The cycle of phases</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Angle from the Sun</th><th>What we see</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>New moon</td><td>0°</td><td>Dark side faces Earth; not visible</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Waxing crescent</td><td>0–90°</td><td>A thin, growing sliver</td></tr>
        <tr><td>First quarter</td><td>90°</td><td>Half of the disc lit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Waxing gibbous</td><td>90–180°</td><td>More than half, still growing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Full moon</td><td>180°</td><td>Entire disc lit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Waning gibbous</td><td>180–270°</td><td>More than half, shrinking</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Third (last) quarter</td><td>270°</td><td>The other half of the disc lit</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Waning crescent</td><td>270–360°</td><td>A thin, shrinking sliver</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Waxing</strong> means growing and <strong>waning</strong> means shrinking. <strong>Gibbous</strong> means more than half. The full
    cycle, called the <strong>synodic month</strong>, takes about 29.5 days.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rising">When the Moon rises</h2>
  <p>
    Because Earth spins once a day, the Moon rises and sets just like the Sun. Its position relative to the Sun decides
    when: a new moon rises with the Sun at dawn, a first-quarter moon rises around noon, a full moon rises at sunset and
    stays up all night, and a third-quarter moon rises around midnight. On average the Moon rises about 50 minutes later
    each day.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hemispheres">North vs. south</h2>
  <p>
    Observers in the Southern Hemisphere see the Moon “upside down” compared with those in the north, so the lit side is
    mirrored: a waxing crescent is lit on the left. Near the equator, crescents can appear lying on their backs, like a
    smile. The phase itself — how much is lit — is the same everywhere on Earth at the same moment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eclipses">Phases are not eclipses</h2>
  <p>
    Earth’s shadow does <em>not</em> cause the phases. Turn on Earth’s shadow in the simulator: it points directly away
    from the Sun, so it can only touch the Moon at full moon. Even then, the Moon’s orbit is tilted about 5° to Earth’s
    orbit, so it usually passes above or below the shadow. When the alignment is just right, we get a
    <strong>lunar eclipse</strong>. Similarly, a <strong>solar eclipse</strong> can only happen at new moon.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas</h2>
  <p>If θ is the angle between the Moon and the Sun as seen from Earth (0° at new moon):</p>
  <code class="formula">Fraction of disc lit  k = (1 − cos θ) / 2</code>
  <code class="formula">Day of the cycle ≈ (θ / 360°) × 29.53 days</code>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Synodic month</dt>
    <dd>The time from one new moon to the next, about 29.53 days.</dd>
    <dt>Sidereal month</dt>
    <dd>The time for the Moon to orbit once relative to the stars, about 27.3 days. It is shorter because Earth also moves around the Sun.</dd>
    <dt>Terminator</dt>
    <dd>The line between the lit and dark parts of the Moon.</dd>
    <dt>Synchronous rotation</dt>
    <dd>The Moon spins once per orbit, so the same face always points toward Earth.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world connections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Calendars:</strong> many calendars, including the Islamic, Hebrew and Chinese calendars, are based fully or partly on lunar phases.</li>
    <li><strong>Tides:</strong> the strongest (spring) tides occur near new and full moon, when the Sun and Moon pull in line.</li>
    <li><strong>Astronomy and photography:</strong> astronomers schedule deep-sky observing around new moon, when the sky is darkest.</li>
    <li><strong>Wildlife:</strong> some animals time behaviour to the lunar cycle — many corals spawn on nights linked to the full moon.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Earth’s shadow causes the phases.”</strong> Phases come from viewing angle; Earth’s shadow only causes eclipses.</li>
    <li><strong>“The Moon is only visible at night.”</strong> It is often visible in daylight, especially around the quarter phases.</li>
    <li><strong>“There is a permanent dark side of the Moon.”</strong> There is a far side we never see, but it gets as much sunlight as the near side — it is fully lit at new moon.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Half of the Moon is always lit by the Sun; phases show how much of that half faces us.</li>
    <li>The cycle takes about 29.5 days: new → first quarter → full → third quarter → new.</li>
    <li>The phase determines when the Moon rises and when it is best seen.</li>
    <li>Eclipses are rare because the Moon’s orbit is tilted, not because shadows cause phases.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: the diagram is not to scale. The real Moon is about 30 Earth diameters away, and the Sun is about 400 times farther still.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
